WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
When Tesla was still a struggling company in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.940
Elon Musk admitted he fired employees&nbsp;
who had the weakest performance reviews.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.693
And he made no apologies for it.

00:00:11.693 --> 00:00:18.600
If you have two boxers of equal ability and&nbsp;
one's much smaller, the big guy's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.438
crush the little guy--obviously. So, the little&nbsp;
guy better have a heck of a lot more skill ...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:29.880
or he is going to get clobbered. So that is why&nbsp;
our standards are high. They're not high because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.320
we believe in being mean to people. They're&nbsp;
high because if they're not high, we will die.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.040
He believes it's critical to&nbsp;
separate the good from the great.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.060
His decision to slash half of Twitter’s staff of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.900
7,500 his first week at the&nbsp;
helm was more controversial.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.140
Did he fire the least effective employees? Did he&nbsp;
even have enough time to figure out who they were?

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.200
The purge continued two weeks later.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.320
In a late-night company-wide email titled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.600
“A fork in the road,” Musk reiterated the&nbsp;
kind of person he wants working for him.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.780
They’re the ones who “Work extremely hardcore.”

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.700
“This will mean working long&nbsp;
hours at high intensity. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.180
exceptional performance will&nbsp;
constitute a passing grade.”

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.400
Anyone who didn't commit by clicking the&nbsp;
enclosed link was forced to leave the company.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.860
Hundreds are believed to have headed out the&nbsp;
door, flooding Twitter with salute emojis.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.800
But Musk assured that “The best people&nbsp;
are staying, so I’m not super worried."

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.160
These are some of the “best&nbsp;
people,” according to the boss.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.660
So, what kind of a person&nbsp;
makes the cut under Elon Musk?

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.860
Probably not the ones wearing these&nbsp;
shirts found at Twitter headquarters.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:51.240
Clip There’s an entire, entire&nbsp;
closet full of hashtag woke t-shirts.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.680
In all seriousness, apparently,&nbsp;
they have to be a bit like him.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.860
He’s the most driven person I think I’ve ever met

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.960
Former NASA astronaut Garrett Reisman&nbsp;
now works as a consultant for SpaceX.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:09.480
Musk has famously slept at Tesla’s&nbsp;
factories during the most challenging times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.760
like in 2018 when Tesla struggled&nbsp;
with production of the Model 3.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.240
He said he was working 120 hours a week.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.260
Although he said the expectations of his staff are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.580
“much less than I demand of myself,”&nbsp;
his team suffered along with him.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.400
They were expected to put in long hours, too.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:36.900
Musk says there was no other way to turn Tesla&nbsp;
around as he told journalist Kara Swisher.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.820
It is absurd that Tesla is alive. Absurd. Absurd.&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.720
So what do you accredit that to?
Excruciating effort.&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.600
By you?
100-hour weeks. By everyone.&nbsp;

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.000
By everyone here at Tesla.
Yes. There wasn’t some other way to do this.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.300
It was through the sheer force of their&nbsp;
blood and sweat and perhaps some tears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:09.600
that Tesla saw the light at the end of&nbsp;
a long, dark tunnel and survived 2018.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.800
He’s now bringing that same work&nbsp;
mentality to Twitter in 2022.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.920
He claims the app is losing $4 million&nbsp;
a day and that he has no other choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.600
but to cut costs and whip Twitter&nbsp;
into shape through extreme effort.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.420
Musk told Twitter staff they are expected&nbsp;
in the office at least 40 hours a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.560
and reportedly warned them to&nbsp;
brace for 80-hour work weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.920
which would be quite the change&nbsp;
from what many are used to.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.440
Senior Twitter engineer Siru Murugesan&nbsp;
was secretly recorded by the conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.120
group Project Veritas admitting&nbsp;
he only worked four hours a week.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:54.660
Musk believes working extraordinarily long&nbsp;
hours gives an extraordinary advantage.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.820
Here’s what he said in 2010.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.700
Work like hell. I mean, you just have to put in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.780
80 to 100-hour weeks every week. All those things&nbsp;
improve the odds of success. If other people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:13.560
putting in 40-hour workweeks and you're putting&nbsp;
in 100-hour workweeks, then even if you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:23.460
the same thing, you know that you will achieve in&nbsp;
four months what it takes them a year to achieve.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.620
He’s also banned working from home for&nbsp;
ALL his companies except in exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.760
circumstances as he believes people&nbsp;
slack off when they aren’t in the office.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.380
Audio leaked to ABC News reveals&nbsp;
that a Twitter employee challenged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.080
him on his mandatory return to office policy.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.820
Even if people return to the office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.940
the offices are separate offices&nbsp;
-- we won't be in person anyways.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.580
Yes, but you can still maximize&nbsp;
the amount of in-person activity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.700
Tesla is not one place either…basically&nbsp;
if you can show up at an office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.500
you do not show up at the office,&nbsp;
resignation accepted -- end of story.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.360
Returning to the office will no longer&nbsp;
include the luxury of free lunches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.220
a common perk at big tech companies.&nbsp;
Musk has cut this from Twitter’s budget.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.140
But it’s not enough to simply work&nbsp;
hard without free food at Elon Musk’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.940
companies. He’s demanding that his&nbsp;
employees be extremely talented.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:23.340
Musk confirmed, “those writing great code&nbsp;
will constitute the majority of our team.”

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.780
Twitter is a software company at its&nbsp;
heart where engineers must be on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:32.220
alert when someone hacks the system,&nbsp;
or when a major bug crashes the system.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.020
And if he wants to turn Twitter into the&nbsp;
“everything app” like what WeChat has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.580
become in China, engineers will be the&nbsp;
ones tasked with adding new features.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.080
He believes a small group of&nbsp;
highly talented engineers can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.040
be more effective than a large group&nbsp;
of engineers who are just pretty good.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.540
He explained during a Q &amp; A with&nbsp;
Twitter employees: “If you look at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.340
say, the Tesla Autopilot AI&nbsp;
team, it’s about 150 engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:04.320
and they’re outperforming teams that they’re&nbsp;
competing against that are 3,000 engineers.”

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.660
So Twitter 2.0 is relying on&nbsp;
a much smaller workforce to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.460
get more done. They also have to deal&nbsp;
with setbacks after Musk’s overhaul.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.780
There’s been a mass drop in revenue&nbsp;
as advertisers have pulled out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.660
unsure of the new direction under the new boss.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.580
Musk tried implementing an $8-a-month&nbsp;
subscription plan for people to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:32.040
verified but had to put it on hold after&nbsp;
some users started impersonating brands.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.720
And now, the company must brace&nbsp;
for what’s to come after Musk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.980
allowed Donald Trump back on Twitter&nbsp;
in his promise to save free speech.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.640
He reversed the ban on Trump&nbsp;
after his Twitter poll showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.400
people narrowly wanted the former president back.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.400
Other tech companies are watching Musk closely.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.020
They’ve suffered in the post-pandemic&nbsp;
world. Meta recently laid off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.140
11,000 workers. Amazon plans to cut thousands&nbsp;
of people, continuing layoffs in 2023.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.920
IF Musk can run a lean ship, and be profitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.200
this will signal to other tech firms&nbsp;
that it’s possible to be lean and green.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.960
But it will not be easy getting there.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.780
It’s hard working for Elon Musk.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.280
Take it from Dolly Singh, the former&nbsp;
head of talent acquisition at SpaceX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:27.285
who witnessed the failure of the third launch of&nbsp;
the Falcon 1, the predecessor to today’s Falcon 9.

00:07:27.285 --> 00:07:31.680
By that point, Musk had burned through the $100&nbsp;
million he put into his private rocket company.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:37.860
Dolly Singh admitted on the question-and-answer&nbsp;
site Quora, “Working with him isn’t a comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:43.980
experience, he is never satisfied with himself so&nbsp;
he is never really satisfied with anyone around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:51.480
him. He pushes himself harder and harder and he&nbsp;
pushes others around him the exact same way. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.360
challenge is that he is a machine and the rest&nbsp;
of us aren’t. So if you work for Elon you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.720
to accept the discomfort. But in that discomfort&nbsp;
is the kind of growth you can’t get anywhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:13.860
and worth every ounce of blood and sweat.”
In the case of SpaceX, persistence paid off.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.540
An investment from a venture capital firm made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.940
possible for SpaceX to attempt a fourth&nbsp;
flight, which was ultimately successful.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.460
Stage separation confirmed!

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:30.000
SpaceX’s achievements are still being written. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.660
the road to get to today has&nbsp;
not been easy for employees.

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:38.220
And neither will the road be easy&nbsp;
for employees at his latest company.

